WEBVTT

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.300
UCLA Moonshot Math Verifier helps you check mathematical papers.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:10.200
It comes as a skill called math-paper-verifier,

00:00:10.440 --> 00:00:13.160
which works with Codex and Claude Code.

00:00:13.660 --> 00:00:16.820
Here, I paste the repository link into Codex

00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:19.160
and ask it to install the skill.

00:00:19.940 --> 00:00:22.660
Codex downloads the files, checks the setup,

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:24.640
and runs the install command.

00:00:25.020 --> 00:00:28.760
Once it's ready, I open a new task to try it out.

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:34.080
For this demo, I'm using A Bound on Partitioning Clusters,

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:36.980
by Daniel Kane and Terence Tao.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.400
I've saved the PDF in a local folder.

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:42.800
I select the Math Verifier skill

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:47.160
and ask it to check the file and give me a findings report.

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:51.920
You can also use LaTeX, Markdown, or plain text.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:54.700
Your paper doesn't need to be online.

00:00:55.507 --> 00:00:58.647
The skill checks the paper in several ways.

00:00:58.927 --> 00:01:01.767
One AI worker reads the whole paper,

00:01:01.907 --> 00:01:04.727
while others check smaller parts in detail.

00:01:05.187 --> 00:01:07.167
A separate worker checks references

00:01:07.167 --> 00:01:09.827
and results from outside sources.

00:01:10.707 --> 00:01:13.567
The findings are then combined into one report.

00:01:14.167 --> 00:01:15.947
A full review takes time.

00:01:16.667 --> 00:01:20.387
For this demo, let's look at the report and one of its findings.

00:01:21.313 --> 00:01:24.093
The report gives an overall result,

00:01:24.393 --> 00:01:28.033
then lists possible problems and where to find them.

00:01:28.373 --> 00:01:33.033
Let's look at the first one, in Remark six on page eight.

00:01:33.413 --> 00:01:37.633
This step in the proof needs a curve that keeps bending upward,

00:01:38.093 --> 00:01:39.733
like the one on the left.

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:43.733
But the curve in this example bends downward instead.

00:01:44.433 --> 00:01:46.913
So this step needs another argument.

00:01:47.833 --> 00:01:50.893
That doesn't mean the paper's main results are wrong.

00:01:51.573 --> 00:01:55.033
I can read the explanation and suggested repair,

00:01:55.773 --> 00:01:58.933
then compare them with the original passage in the paper.

00:01:59.933 --> 00:02:03.793
These findings still need a mathematician's judgment.

00:02:04.113 --> 00:02:06.973
And if the review finds no problems,

00:02:07.033 --> 00:02:09.753
that doesn't prove the paper is correct.

00:02:10.713 --> 00:02:13.413
You can also start with the paper's web link,

00:02:13.653 --> 00:02:15.113
focus on one part,

00:02:15.313 --> 00:02:18.793
or look for examples that would disprove the main claims.

00:02:19.653 --> 00:02:22.733
Visit the UCLA Moonshot Math Verifier website

00:02:22.733 --> 00:02:25.493
for the setup guide and download.
